# PART 3 — WORKSPACE ARCHITECTURE

## 3.1 Workspace definition

One Workspace represents one company, institution or team.

A Workspace contains:

- One Admin.
- Multiple Employees.
- Tasks.
- Task assignments.
- Status Requests.
- Meetings.
- Notifications.
- Subscription information.
- Usage information.
- Audit history.

Each customer gets a separate Workspace.

The subscription belongs to the Workspace, not to an individual user.

All users within a Workspace inherit its subscription entitlement, but their role-based permissions remain separate.

## 3.2 Workspace rules

Admin:
- Can manage Workspace employees.
- Can create and assign tasks.
- Can view Workspace-wide task information.
- Can create Status Requests when the subscription permits.
- Can generate Google Meet links when the subscription permits.
- Can view subscription details and usage.

Employee:
- Can view their assigned tasks.
- Can update their task status.
- Can view requests assigned to them.
- Can respond to assigned requests when entitled.
- Can join meetings for which they are invited.
- Cannot manage subscriptions.
- Cannot create Status Requests.
- Cannot generate Google Meet links.

All Workspace access must be enforced by the backend.

## 3.3 Tenant isolation

Use top-level Firestore collections with a mandatory `workspaceId` field wherever applicable.

Every relevant backend query must verify Workspace ownership.

Never allow an Admin or Employee from one Workspace to access another Workspace's records.

